## Context

Tickets 0551-0556 (and 0565, filed alongside this one) each build and test
one module of the conductor in isolation, against fixtures and virtual
time: the ledger/tick, election/lease, extract shim, the v2 schema, and
(once 0565 lands) the segmentation write path. None of them assembles a
real, running process.

**Since 2026-09-02 the process this assembles is the writer, not a server
wearing a second hat** (DECISIONS.md 2026-09-02, both entries; SPEC.md §5.2.5).
Four process roles, not two: P0, a query-serving server; the conductor, the
sole writer of derived state, a process of its own, spawned by whichever server
finds the lease unheld; one pipeline worker, which fetches, drives the
embedding service and writes nothing; and the embedding service (ticket 0575),
which holds the machine's one copy of each embedder generation. Three
consequences this assembly must carry rather than settle by default in code:

- A P0 must not link the write role at all. What a server writes is control
  state and nothing else — the pause row, intent rows, its own liveness row —
  in a table of its own. "Write-free" names the query path and the derived
  stores, not the binary; the stronger phrasing was withdrawn the day it was
  written.
- The conductor is NOT run-to-drain and does not exit on pause. It lives while
  any P0 lives, because it owns the reconcile tick, and it exits when no P0
  liveness row is live. The worker alone drains and exits; pause means zero
  workers and the tick keeps running its removal branch. A conductor that
  exited on an empty queue would be re-spawned by the next election check, N
  times over, forever.
- Embedding is an out-of-process call. Neither the conductor nor the worker
  loads a model, and the worker never spawns the service.

Fault isolation is the point of the split: a build crash must degrade to a
pipeline outage and never to a search outage — upstream's #37, the only live
item on `SYNC.md`'s tracker.

This ticket is the assembly point, and the author's stated acceptance bar
for it: point the assembled process at a fixture library (tranche 1's
synthetic ~50-item library, served through its record/replay fake of the
local API) and get a real, queryable `search-index-v2.sqlite` out the
other end — entries, slabs, passages, and vectors populated by an actual
run of the pipeline, not by unit tests calling internal methods directly.

This is explicitly short of "the real thing" (the author's actual Zotero
library, replacing or sitting beside v1 in production). Two further gaps
stay out of this ticket's scope, flagged for separate decisions: wiring
`search-index-v2.sqlite` into the actual MCP query path (0556 marked this
out of its own scope too), and any migration/coexistence story for a user
who already has a v1 index. Both need the author's call on shape before
they're ticket-sized.

## The two probes that gate the boundary

DECISIONS.md 2026-09-02 demotes two things from ruling to probe, "both before
0566 commits the boundary", and both are now Blocked-by:

- 0576 — whether a Claude-Desktop-hosted server can spawn a child at all, and
  under what environment. The isolation benefit the split was ruled on exists
  only if the spawn works, and nothing has verified it.
- 0577 — the service ceiling sized for two resident generations. A ceiling
  pinned at one model rules SPEC.md §5.2.7's dual residency out by arithmetic.

Two further items from the same entry are rulings rather than probes, and land
here rather than in tickets of their own: the fresh-install bootstrap (Action 7)
and the conductor's lifetime mechanism (Action 3). The worker's heap limit,
the third item this ticket's 05:41Z log entry called a probe, is likewise a
ruling and belongs to ticket 0554, which owns the worker.

## Relevant files

- `SPEC.md` §5.2.5 — the four-role topology: the lease-held conductor as its
  own process, its liveness-row lifetime, the fresh-install bootstrap
  (temporary file, rename, then lease, then work), the run-to-drain worker
  under `nice 19` and a heap limit, the full-duplex pipe ("the conductor
  drains arriving windows and records before blocking on any write of work
  orders, in both directions"), orphan repair (stdin EOF on the worker
  side, lease-loss kill on the conductor side), and the embedding service.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.9 — what each process class costs after the re-cut, and the
  three ceilings left needing a re-pin.
- Ticket 0575 — the embedding service boundary this assembly calls into.
- Fork `src/index.ts` — v1's current entry point, for the pattern this
  ticket's new path should follow structurally; this ticket ADDS a
  conductor-aware path, it does not replace or modify v1's, per the
  parallel-schema directive that shaped 0556.
- Everything tranches 1-3, 0556, and 0565 built: `ledger.ts`, `lease.ts`,
  `election.ts`, `conductor.ts`, `orphan.ts`, the extract-stage modules,
  0556's schema additions, 0565's segmentation loop. Read what actually
  landed — this ticket is the integration point, so its accuracy depends
  on reading the real merged code, not this description.
- Tranche 1's fixtures (`tests/fixtures/local-api-replay.ts`,
  `synthetic-library.ts`) — the fixture library and the fake local API
  this ticket's acceptance test points the assembled process at.

## Actions

1. A real process entry point for the CONDUCTOR — its own process, not a
   role a P0 takes on — which opens `search-index-v2.sqlite` (the file 0556
   wires) alongside, never instead of, v1's `search-index.sqlite`.
2. Wire election (tranche 2) so that genuinely separate P0 instances (real OS
   processes, not simulated connections in one test process — tranche 2's
   own soak test used separate SQLite connections, not separate processes;
   this ticket is where that gap closes) spawn exactly one conductor: the
   election is among writer candidates, and the server that finds the lease
   unheld spawns it. Verify against 0576's recipe on the host that needs one.
3. The conductor's lifetime: it exits when no P0 liveness row is live, and
   never on an empty queue. Each P0 keeps a liveness row in the same `leases`
   table on the same TTL.
4. Spawn the pipeline worker as a real child process on ledger-queue
   non-empty, run-to-drain, exit at empty — per SPEC's topology, not a
   thread or an in-process simulation. Run-to-drain is the worker's property
   and only the worker's.
5. Wire the full-duplex stdio pipe between conductor and worker exactly
   as SPEC describes it (windows and records flowing one way, work orders
   the other, neither side blocking the other's drain).
6. Make the local-API source swappable: a real Zotero local API for
   production use, tranche 1's record/replay fixture for a dry run
   against the fixture library — same conductor code, different source.
7. Fresh-install bootstrap: the lease lives in a file that does not exist yet
   and no server creates the schema. The first conductor writes an empty
   schema to a temporary file, renames it into place, and only then takes the
   lease and only then works, so two conductors racing on a fresh install
   lose nothing — neither has written a row before the rename decides.
8. Connect tranche 2's orphan-repair primitives (`orphan.ts`) to the real
   process lifecycles established here — they exist and are tested in
   isolation; this is where they're actually called.
9. Pause: pause means zero workers, and the tick keeps running its removal
   branch. Neither the conductor nor the tick exits on pause.

## Test

- The acceptance test: point the assembled process at tranche 1's
  fixture library through the replay fake, run it to completion (or to a
  stable idle state under virtual/accelerated time), and assert
  `search-index-v2.sqlite` ends up populated — entries, slabs, passages,
  vectors — and directly queryable (raw SQL against the file is enough
  here; wiring the MCP query path is a separate, later ticket).
- A multi-process election test: launch genuinely separate OS processes
  (not simulated connections) against one data directory, kill the
  conductor uncatchably, confirm another takes over within TTL + cadence —
  the same property tranche 2 tested, now over real processes.
- A bootstrap race: two conductors started at once against an empty data
  directory. Neither loses a row, and the rename decides.

## Verification

- [ ] Election works across genuinely separate OS processes
- [ ] The conductor survives an empty queue and exits when the last P0
      liveness row expires — the spawn-loop failure mode has a test, not a
      comment
- [ ] A P0 links no write path to derived state; the only rows it writes are
      control rows (positive control: a P0 asked to write a derived row fails)
- [ ] Neither the conductor nor the worker loads a model; embedding crosses
      the process boundary
- [ ] The pipe survives a worker crash and a conductor handover without
      corrupting in-flight work
- [ ] A full run against the fixture library completes and the resulting
      `search-index-v2.sqlite` is queryable by direct inspection
- [ ] A build crash takes the pipeline down and leaves queries answering —
      the fault isolation the split was ruled for (upstream #37)
- [ ] Orphan repair fires in both directions under a real process kill,
      not only in the unit-level simulation tranche 2 shipped

## Invariants

- v1's `search-index.sqlite` and its query path are untouched — this is
  an additional capability, not a replacement.
- Upstream tests green throughout.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Both probes (0576, 0577) answered and recorded BEFORE the boundary is
      committed in code; a negative on 0576 goes to DECISIONS.md as a
      re-ruling question rather than to this ticket as a workaround
- [ ] A runnable process assembly merged on a fork branch
- [ ] An end-to-end index build against the fixture library succeeds and
      is verified queryable
- [x] The open question in this ticket's log (whether this needed to wait
      on 0555) is resolved and recorded, whichever way it went — settled
      2026-09-01: no wait, the acceptance assertions are order-insensitive
